# SIMF — .gitignore

# ── Production secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# deploy/ holds five set-env-*.ps1 (api, cp, web, edge, webapp) plus
# clean-env.ps1. Those five ARE the operator-facing files: they carry the real
# connection strings, the JWT signing key, both encryption keys, the SMTP
# password and the AI keys — 18 secret-marked entries in set-env-api.ps1 alone.
#
# They were tracked ON PURPOSE for a period, and this text used to say so. That
# is REVERSED as of 2026-08-22, at the owner's instruction: all five are now
# untracked and operator-supplied. They stay on disk beside clean-env.ps1, which
# reads `set-env-$Target.ps1` from $PSScriptRoot — so a local deploy still works,
# while a FRESH CLONE deliberately does not have them.
#
# Untracking is NOT rotation. Every secret these files ever carried is still
# readable in git history, permanently. Rotate anything that reached a remote you
# do not control — that is the step that actually closes the exposure.
#
# Nothing in CI depends on them: azure-pipelines.yml only names them in a comment
# about not staging them into artifacts, and a deployment job never clones.
# The set-env-*.ps1 scripts are TEMPLATES and are tracked: every secret is
# declared but ships EMPTY, so an operator can see which keys exist without
# the repository carrying one. Only the FILLED overlays are ignored.
deploy/set-env-*.local.ps1
set-env-prod.ps1
set-env-*.local.ps1

# ── Runtime file store — personal data, NEVER commit ───────────────
# App_Data/files is the filesystem storage root (FilesystemFileStorageProvider),
# so anything a local run uploads lands here: avatars, ID-document scans,
# speaker photos. 55 such files had been committed by accident and were
# untracked on 2026-08-05; they are runtime artifacts, referenced by nothing in
# source, and an ID scan has no business in source control. Ignored so a
# `git add` on the project directory cannot sweep them back in.
src/Backend/SIMF.Api/App_Data/files/
src/Backend/SIMF.Api/App_Data/recordings/

# ── .NET build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# `bin/` above is a .NET build-output rule, but it matches ANY directory called
# bin at any depth — including `tool/conventions/bin/`, which is a DART package
# entrypoint, i.e. SOURCE. That silently kept the convention checker's `main()`
# out of the repository: the file existed on the machine that wrote it, the gate
# passed there, and `dart run bin/simf_conventions.dart --check` in
# azure-pipelines.yml would fail on any clean checkout with "Could not find
# file". The gate answering the customer's code review could not run.
#
# The negation must re-include the DIRECTORY, not just the file: git cannot
# re-include anything whose parent directory is excluded, so `!.../bin/x.dart`
# alone does nothing.
!tool/conventions/bin/
!tool/conventions/bin/**
artifacts/
publish/
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb

# ── Visual Studio / Rider ──────────────────────────────────────────
.vs/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# ── Flutter / Dart ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
.pub-cache/
.pub/
*.iml
src/MobileApp/build/
Pods/
.gradle/
local.properties
# Golden-comparison output. `flutter test` writes the four diff PNGs per failing
# golden here; they are generated artefacts of a FAILING run, never an input to
# one. 48 of them had been committed and were stale against a long-superseded
# revision, so anyone grepping the directory was reading obsolete debris
# (STALE-GOLDEN-ARTIFACTS). The masters live in test/golden/goldens/.
src/Mobile/simf_app/test/golden/failures/

# ── Secrets & environment ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Production config is applied via Machine-scope env vars (set-env-*.ps1);
# real secret values are NEVER committed. Only the placeholder template is.
appsettings.Production.json
appsettings.*.Local.json
*.env
.env
secrets.json
set-env-*.local.ps1

# ── Claude Code local settings ─────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberately NARROW: each per-machine or generated path is named, and
# `.claude/` itself is never ignored. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so a blanket `.claude/` rule could not be undone by a
# later negation — it would silently swallow a shared config (settings.json,
# agents/, commands/) and the `git add` would just do nothing.
.claude/settings.local.json
# Skills vendored from anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins. Generic knowledge-work
# tooling installed per-developer, not SIMF source; tracking them would impose
# one person's toolset on every clone.
.claude/skills/
# Transient worktrees. Each is a FULL working checkout of this repo, so a stray
# `git add` here would recurse the entire tree into a commit.
.claude/worktrees/
# The lockfile pinning the skills above. Ignored, not tracked, because the
# thing it pins is itself untracked — a lockfile for content nobody clones is
# noise. If shared skills are ever wanted, track BOTH this and .claude/skills/,
# not one of them.
/skills-lock.json

# ── Logs & temp ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime log output only — root-anchored so source-tree `Logs/` folders
# (e.g. SIMF.Application/Logs, SIMF.Api/Endpoints/Admin/Logs) stay tracked.
*.log
/logs/
*.tmp

# ── UI-audit scratch (generated screenshots, run logs, helper scripts) ─
/.ui-audit/

# ── OS files ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Repo-root scratch notes. txt.txt was tracked and carried a plaintext
# production super-admin credential (twice) plus demo passwords; removed
# 2026-07-30. Scratch files do not belong in the repo at all.
txt.txt
# SEC-NOTES (2026-07-30) — myComment.txt is the owner's hand-written fix-list.
# Untracked, NOT deleted: it is owner-authored, other work still references it,
# and it stays on disk. Untracked because a plaintext working-notes file at the
# repo root has no review gate — the next paste into it lands in a commit with
# nobody reading it, which is how txt.txt came to carry a production credential.
# Requires `git rm --cached myComment.txt` once, or the tracked copy survives
# this ignore rule.
myComment.txt
/*.scratch.txt
# HANDOFF-CONTINUE-HERE.md is a per-session scratch handoff. Its own line 5 reads
# "This file is UNTRACKED on purpose (do not commit it)" — but it was committed on
# 2026-07-26 and had drifted three weeks out of date, still naming a branch and a
# set of "remaining work" that no longer existed. A file that describes live state
# cannot be maintained from inside a commit history; honour what it says about
# itself and keep it on disk only. Untracked 2026-08-12, NOT deleted.
# Requires `git rm --cached HANDOFF-CONTINUE-HERE.md` once, or the tracked copy
# survives this ignore rule.
HANDOFF-CONTINUE-HERE.md

# Editor/manual backups, including the timestamped form (CLAUDE.md.bak-20260812).
# A backup of a tracked file is a second copy of it that git does not diff, so it
# drifts silently — and for the governing rules files that everything else in this
# repo defers to, a stale second copy is worse than none. The tracked file plus its
# history already is the backup. Kept on disk, never committed.
*.bak
*.bak-*

# Test run artefacts. A .trx captures the whole test console log; two of
# these were tracked at 707 MB and 564 MB, so every clone paid 1.2 GB for
# stale output. Nothing reads them.
TestResults/
*.trx

# Python bytecode cache from the diagram scripts under tools/diagrams.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Store submission masters: 1080x2400 RGBA device captures, kept as the
# source for store-screenshots/play-ready/ but never uploaded (Play caps the
# aspect ratio at 2:1 and rejects alpha on screenshots). Large; not tracked.
store-screenshots/raw-captures/
